. The side table is derivable from named datoms via resolve-name — indeed §1 says \"lexical positions are derived by resolve-name.\" So the side table is derived data shipped for diagnostics; acceptable if explicitly derived and not authoritative, and the design says so. Mild tension, and the compliance table acknowledges it (\"Strained: diagnostics\"). Reasonably honest.\n- Loaded image state: \"loaded images are explicit values; the linker must supply the missing-code event\" — acknowledged as strained. The `:code-aliases` column in VM state mirrors the semantic VM. Fine.\n- Frame environment: \"frames require a lift for completion and exported continuations (§4, B4)\" — acknowledged. `:load-free` falling through to store/primitives/modules mirrors the named resolve-var. \"Position-based locals never fall through to store keys\" — explicit. Clean.\n- Request emission: park + request record onto request stream, data only, no callback retained. Clean, but future.\n- Host boundary is stream boundary: unsupported classes are rejected as qualified outcomes — matches \"a host without an implementation of the effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a correct outcome.\" Clean.\n\nCompliance-table honesty: the row \"I: executable code sharing | canonical image hash, verified load, stream request and response (§2, §7.2)\" — stream request/response is §7.2 future/B6. Within the B phases, sharing is: B5's stream-forwarding test. The table doesn't cite B5. Slight overclaim → P2 or P3. Also \"Everything is a continuation | parked machine state is explicit data and resumes via stream-carried events (§4, §7.2)\" — §7.2 is future here too, but §4's park/resume is B4-real. Fine.\n\n**Item 3: D1-D7.**\n\n- D1 normalizer: §1 \"this normalizer is defined and tested in B0, not assumed to be an existing repo utility\" and B0 freezes it, consistent. Serves parity, indirectly supports invariant I (cross-host same normalized result). Consistent.\n- D2 lossless-DAG dropped: §7.3 non-goal, §1 \"a second lossless fact structure is not persisted,\" §2/§3 derived. Consistent. Check residual text: §3 \"the executable image may have debug node hashes and named source references\" — diagnostic metadata, not a DAG; consistent with §7.3 (\"binder diagnostics... derived into the executable image as needed\"). OK.\n- D3 descriptor: §2 defines the descriptor, raw Bytes slots, scalar domain, lift, rejection. B1 completion includes rejection fixtures. Consistent. One check: §2 says the descriptor declares \"one lift morphism from `:yin.debruijn.code/*` to `:yin.code/*`\" but §2 also says \"persistent publication and discovery are out of scope for B0-B6.\" A dimension descriptor that is defined and exported but not persistently published — fine (recent commit: \"state that the dimension descriptor is defined, not published\").\n- D4 environment leak: §1 \"the named VM's environment leak is a separate defect,\" §5, B4/B5 fixture restrictions. Consistent throughout. Surprising to owner? It defers a real named-VM bug outside these phases; the design flags it as a defect to be fixed separately — fine, but no phase owns that fix. That could \"surprise the owner later\": cross-program park/resume parity tests are restricted until a fix no phase delivers exists. P3.\n- D5 hash identity: §2 \"identity of a code image is the hash of the canonical position-based instruction vector,\" §8 D5 \"`:call-hash H` names the canonical executable image of a definition unit; a recursive component is one hashed unit. Projection fingerprints never become H.\" §2 consistent: \"projection fingerprints may be recorded as metadata... but are not the executable cache key.\" Consistent. But \"definition unit\" is not defined anywhere in the document — what is the definition unit that gets hashed as H? A whole image? A segment? D5 says a recursive component is \"one hashed unit\" — §7.2's SCC principle. But the granularity of H in `:call-hash` (per top-level def? per segment?) is underspecified and deferred to B6. Does it block B0? No — B0 is normalizer + corpus. Fine.\n- D6 linker principles: §7.2, consistent. Name environment \"value or stream\" — datom.world says streams carry values; a value-retained name environment inside the linker process is fine because the linker is a process between streams and \"consumes executable images and a value-retained name environment\" — value-retained is explicit data, not global. OK.\n- D7 coexistence: §1 exit criteria \"the owner may instead default to the frame VM while keeping the semantic VM,\" §8 D7. Consistent. Note: \"frame VM\" is the third name for the same thing (elsewhere \"sibling VM,\" \"de Bruijn VM,\" \"executable VM\") — minor terminology drift, P3? Actually check: §1 \"the owner may instead default to the frame VM\" — \"frame VM\" appears only there and in §6 B3 \"frame VM kernel.\" Minor.\n\nUnderspecified enough to block B0? B0 = normalizer + frozen corpus. D1 gives its spec. §1 says error comparison uses message + normalized ex-data. Fine. Nothing blocks B0 — design claims \"no owner decision blocks B0,\" agree.\n\nSurprises: (a) hash-based retrieval is not built until B6, and B6's schema is deferred — the owner may expect \"sharing code over the dao.stream linker\" to work after B5, but what works after B5 is only image forwarding; the *linker* (requests/responses by hash) is future. The design says so, but the invariant-I compliance row obscures it. (b) D4's leak fix has no owning phase.\n\n**Item 4: Internal consistency and form.**\n\n- 80 cols: no line over 80 (grep clean). Em-dashes: none. Status line 3: \"Status: design; not implemented\" ✓. ASCII tables: §1 compliance table uses ASCII +---. §8 uses numbered lists. Model-routing text: none found (no mention of models). Form is clean.\n- Residual contradictions:\n  - §2 \"the new code namespace copies its small framing and length-prefix helpers and defines its own scalar tag table\" vs B1 \"must not change: yin.vm.code, :yin.code/*, projection encoder\" — consistent (copies, does not touch).\n  - §1 says \"B5 uses a fresh VM instance for normal parity\" and B5 says nothing about a fresh instance; §5 says \"a named VM instance used for B5 must start from a fixed initial environment.\" Consistent enough.\n  - Phase list: §2 says \"out of scope for B0-B6\"; §7.3 \"out of scope for B0-B6\"; §6 defines B0-B5 only, B6 is described in §7.2. So B6 exists as a phase but has no file box in §6. §6 header says \"each phase has a file box, a must-not-change list, completion criteria, and... verification\" — B6 is missing that box in §6; §7.2 gives B6 completion requirements (\"completion requires hash-addressed load, cycle-safe manifest...\"). Minor structural inconsistency: B6's phase spec lives in §7 rather than §6. P3.\n  - B3 completion: \"plus the section 1 benchmark gate for throughput, allocation, image size, load time\" — but §1 says the benchmark report \"is informational, not an acceptance condition,\" and D7 says \"B3 reports benchmark numbers only.\" Contradiction: B3 calls it a \"gate\" (an acceptance condition) while §1/D7 call it informational. \"Benchmark gate\" vs \"not an acceptance condition\" — this is a real residual contradiction. P2? Doesn't break the invariants but contradicts D7/§1 within the document. Call it P2 (significant internal inconsistency: B3 completion cannot simultaneously require the gate and treat the report as informational). Actually, the term \"gate\" might mean a \"checkpoint at which numbers are reported.\" But \"completion requires... benchmark gate\" reads as an acceptance condition. Flag as P2 or P3. It creates ambiguity in B3's acceptance criteria and the document is an executable spec, so P2? The review defines P2 as \"significant gap.\" A contradiction between B3's completion criterion and §1/D7 is significant for implementers. Call it P2.\n  - §1 \"the executable VM promises equivalence with the named semantic VM only after normalization\" and §5, consistent.\n  - §4 \"may implement existing VM protocols without adding methods\" — fine.\n  - Compliance table row widths/formatting: row \"Interpretation semantics|\" is missing a space before the pipe (line 93: `| Interpretation semantics| Named...`). Cosmetic ASCII-table alignment issue — column border misalignment? Check: `+--------------------------+` is 26 chars; \"Interpretation semantics\" is 24 chars + `|` immediately = cell is 25 chars vs 26. Row misaligned. P3 form issue.\n  - §2 line 148: \"This is deliberately distinct from `yin.vm.debruijn/encode-value`.\" — verified encode-value exists ✓.\n  - §7.2 \"B3 already makes load and continuation state explicit, and B4 carries park and resume as data transitions\" — consistent with B3/B4.\n  - §3 B2 \"lift(adapt(lower x), side-table) = canonical-vector(lower x)\" vs §2 \"the lift uses diagnostic binder names or synthesized names\" and B2 \"with synthesized names, the lift result is alpha-equivalent to lower x\" — slight tension: the equation says it equals the canonical vector of lower x (which has original names), while the synthesized-names sentence says only alpha-equivalent. If the side table has the original binder names, lift is exact; if synthesized, alpha-equivalent. The equation as written requires the original-name side table. B2's sentence covers both cases. Minor wording tension, P3.\n  - §6 B1 completion list has odd line wrapping (\"malformed rows,\\nout-of-range\") — content is fine.\n  - §2 \"persistent publication and discovery are out of scope for B0-B6\" vs §7.2/B6 \"completion requires hash-addressed load\" — hash-addressed load implies some discovery/retrieval mechanism in B6. Tension: publication/discovery excluded through B6, yet B6 requires hash-addressed load. If the image arrives via stream (push), B6 can have hash-addressed load without persistent publication. Probably consistent, but worth flagging as P3 underspecification.\n\n- Phases that depend on dropped items: D2 dropped the lossless DAG; does any phase still reference it? B2 references the side table (kept as diagnostics). §7.3 non-goal. Clean.\n\n**Item 5: Top-3 risks to \"share code over dao.stream\":**\n1. Pull-by-hash retrieval is deferred and underspecified (B6 request schema DEFERRED; publication/discovery out of scope for B0-B6): after B5, sharing only works when the sender pushes bytes; \"any yin.vm can retrieve given H\" is not realized in any phase.\n2. Cross-host scalar-domain mismatch: identical H is only guaranteed for the common scalar domain; images using ratio/bigint/char reject on hosts that lack the class — rejection is specified, but there is no negotiation/downgrade path, so real cross-host code sharing silently narrows to the lowest-common-denominator domain.\n3. Free-name resolution divergence: the image hash excludes free-env; identical H can behave differently across hosts or fail with unbound names until B6 dependency closure arrives; no load-time check verifies that B supplies the image's free names.\n\nActually, risk 3: can B check free names at load time? The image's `:load-free` operands are hashed and enumerable, so the validator *could* check resolvability against free-env/store/primitives/modules at load time. The design doesn't specify that. That's a concrete P2/P3 finding: add load-time free-name resolvability check, or explicitly declare runtime failure as the contract. The document says \"free names continue to be resolved by name until the stream linker supplies dependency closures\" — acknowledged. But a defined outcome for a missing free name on the receiving host is not stated. P3 with a minimal fix.\n\nAdditional item 1 question: \"what does B do if the image uses scalar classes B lacks\" — answered: rejects with qualified unsupported-value before execution (§2). \"How does B obtain the image when it only knows H\" — gap: no mechanism until B6; §7.2 parking/request is the planned mechanism but future. \"What do free names inside the image resolve to on B\" — B's own free-env/store/primitives/modules via the resolve-var order; no closure shipping.\n\nVerdict: SOUND WITH CHANGES.\n\nP1: none? Does anything fail an invariant outright? The compliance-table I row cites future §7.2 mechanism — the owner's invariant is \"can easily share code over the dao.stream linker.\" B5 demonstrates stream-forwarding sharing, so the invariant is met in the push sense; the linker (by name/hash) is future. The design is mostly honest about this. No P1. B3 \"benchmark gate\" contradiction — internal, P2.\n\nFindings list:\n- P2-1: §6 B3 \"completion requires... plus the section 1 benchmark gate\" contradicts §1 \"the report is informational, not an acceptance condition\" and D7 \"B3 reports benchmark numbers only.\" Fix: replace \"benchmark gate\" with \"benchmark report (informational).\"\n- P2-2: Compliance-table row I cites \"stream request and response (§2, §7.2)\" — request/response is B6/future, and B6's request schema is DEFERRED in §8; the only real sharing mechanism within the phases is B5's image forwarding. Fix: cite B5's stream-forwarding test for present satisfaction and mark §7.2 as future.\n- P2-3 (or P3): retrieval by hash — \"how B obtains the image when B knows only H\" is not answered by any phase; §2 excludes publication/discovery through B6 while B6 requires \"hash-addressed load.\" Fix: state that within B0-B5 sharing is sender-push over a stream, and hash-addressed pull arrives with B6 whose request schema is deferred.\n\nHmm, P2-3 and P2-2 overlap. Merge or keep separate: P2-2 is table honesty, P2-3 is pull-path specification gap + internal tension (\"out of scope for B0-B6\" vs B6 completion's \"hash-addressed load\"). Keep both.\n\n- P3: free-name resolvability on the receiving host is not a load-time check and has no defined outcome; minimal fix: one sentence saying a missing free name is a qualified runtime error outcome (or a validator check).\n- P3: \"the image records the scalar classes it uses\" — clarify whether this manifest is derived from the hashed instruction vector (recommended per derive-don't-persist) or is a separate hashed/unhashed field.\n- P3: B6 has no §6 phase box despite §6's claim that \"each phase has a file box...\"; B6's completion criteria live in §7.2.\n- P3: B2's lift equation `lift(adapt(lower x), side-table) = canonical-vector(lower x)` holds only with original binder names; with synthesized names only alpha-equivalence (as the same section also says). Tighten the equation's wording.\n- P3: D4 leak fix has no owning phase/design; fixture restrictions could persist indefinitely.\n- P3: compliance-table row \"Interpretation semantics|\" misalignment (cell 25 vs 26 chars).\n- P3: terminology drift \"frame VM\" (§1, B3) vs \"sibling VM\"/\"de Bruijn VM.\"\n\nClean checks: form (80 cols, no em-dashes, status line, no model-routing text, ASCII tables); source references verified (resolve-name public at debruijn.cljc:606; index-frame/build-node/project-node private; encode-value exists; vector-operand-table at code.cljc:194; segment-scope public, closure-ranges/layout-conforms? private at completion.cljc; load-vector/:code-aliases/:yin.code/hash/jing/segment-key/sha256 verified; :yin.k/requires at completion.cljc; UTF-16 surrogate guard in the projection verified); projection claims (tail? dropped, binder names outside fingerprint, numeric normalization) verified; D1-D7 consistent with the architect's findings; no callbacks/global state/shared mutable state in the mechanisms; park/request design matches host-boundary doctrine.\n\nTimestamp: need current GMT. Today is 2026-09-21, creation is 19:08 GMT. I'm... can't run `date`? Is a read-only date command allowed? In plan mode, read-only commands are permitted; `date` is read-only. But earlier the shell was rejected for awk (probably because of writing? no — the rejection was blanket). Let me try `date -u`. Actually the rejection message said read-only commands can still be run; awk isn't on the