 P3-5 — D4's leak fix has no owner\nSection: §8 D4, §1, §5, B4.\nQuoted: \"treat it as a separate named-VM defect outside these phases; retain\nthe stated fixture restriction until fixed.\"\nProblem: no phase or referenced design owns the fix, so the park/resume\nfixture restriction may persist indefinitely and B4/B5 parity stays narrowed\nwithout a tracked path to widening it.\nSmallest fix: add to DEFERRED: \"a named-VM environment-leak fix design owns\nD4's release condition.\"\n\n### P3-6 — Form: one misaligned table row\nSection: §1 compliance table.\nQuoted: \"| Interpretation semantics|\" — the cell is 25 characters against a\n26-character column; the border does not line up.\nSmallest fix: pad to \"Interpretation semantics |\".\n\n### P3-7 — Terminology drift: \"frame VM\"\nSection: §1, §6 B3.\nQuoted: \"the owner may instead make the frame VM the default\" and \"B3: frame\nVM kernel,\" while the rest of the document says \"sibling VM\" / \"de Bruijn VM\"\n/ \"executable VM.\"\nSmallest fix: pick one name (de Bruijn VM) and use it in §1 and the B3\nheading.\n\n---\n\n## ANSWERS\n\n### 1. Invariant I, end to end (host A JVM lowers, host B Dart runs)\n\n1. **A lowers.** A holds named `:yin/*` datoms plus the optional projection\n   fingerprint. The B2 adapter runs `yin.vm.linearize/lower`, rewrites each\n   `:var` to `:load-bound [depth pos]` or `:load-free name`, and replaces\n   closure parameter vectors with arity plus body refs. Scope is validated\n   against the frame-arity vector. Covered by §3/B2. No gap.\n2. **A hashes.** B1 computes H over the canonical positional instruction\n   vector: pc-indexed tuples, refs resolved to pcs, descriptor hash, exact\n   scalar bytes; binder names and provenance stay in the side table outside H.\n   Alpha-equivalent programs on A and elsewhere yield the same H. Covered by\n   §2. No gap for the common scalar domain.\n3. **A publishes over the stream.** Within B0–B5 this is sender push: the\n   image bytes plus claimed hash travel as plain stream data. B5's completion\n   requires exactly this cross-lane transfer. Covered, but see P2-3: if B only\n   knows H, no phase supplies the bytes.\n4. **B verifies.** B recomputes the hash from the received canonical vector\n   (same canonical form as `load-image` / `jing/segment-key`, §2), refuses on\n   mismatch, and the B1 validator re-walks every body's arity and\n   enclosing-body chain, rejecting hand-built `[:load-bound [5 0]]` images\n   (§3). Covered; this is the design's strongest part.\n5. **Missing scalar class on B.** \"A receiving host that lacks a class refuses\n   the image with a qualified `unsupported-value` outcome before execution;\n   this is a host-boundary outcome, not a stream gap\" (§2). Covered and\n   consistent with datom.world's \"a host with no implementation emits a\n   qualified unsupported result.\" Residual: the class manifest's own status is\n   ambiguous (P3-2), and refusal is terminal, so real sharing narrows to the\n   common scalar domain with no negotiation path.\n6. **Free names on B.** `:load-free` resolves in B's own `free-env`, store,\n   primitives, and module registry via the existing `resolve-var` order (§4).\n   Same H can behave differently or fail unbound on B; dependency closure is\n   B6 (P3-1). Unstated assumption: B's initial environment supplies the same\n   free names A's did; B5 fixtures must fix the name environment for the\n   cross-lane test to mean anything.\n7. **B runs.** The B3 frame VM executes with positional frames, closures\n   capturing the persistent frame stack, nil-fill/drop-extras call convention.\n   Results agree with A under the B0 normalizer. Covered.\n\nConclusion: the invariant holds for push-sharing of self-contained images in\nthe common scalar domain, tested by B5. The \"obtain given only H\" leg and the\nfree-name closure leg are honestly labeled future (§7.2, B6), but the §1\ncompliance table overstates their present status (P2-2).\n\n### 2. datom.world invariants\n\n- **No hidden global state**: clean. VM state is one explicit map (§4);\n  free-env is \"the VM's initial environment value, fixed for one VM instance\";\n  \"No cache is specified by B0 through B5; any later cache is an explicit\n  value keyed by the canonical image hash\" (§2).\n- **No implicit control flow**: clean. \"Steps classify explicit data; missing\n  code parks and emits a request.\"\n- **No callbacks**: clean. \"No loader or callback is invoked\"; §7.2 \"The\n  request token, parked continuation, and response are data; no callback is\n  retained.\" The B4 \"frame-aware completion adapter\" is a datom.world adapter\n  (a map of transforms), not a callback holder.\n- **No shared mutable state**: clean. Frames, stores, continuations are\n  persistent transition data; the named `bind-params` \"remains name-keyed and\n  unchanged.\"\n- **No layer collapse**: clean. Projection, lowerer, VM, linker are separate\n  interpreters; the linker \"is a process between streams.\"\n- **No assumed graphs**: clean. Body ranges and refs are validated before\n  execution (§3, two independent checks).\n- **Host boundary is a stream boundary**: clean. Refusals are qualified stream\n  outcomes; the linker's \"physical placement is not a linker decision.\"\n- **Derive, don't persist**: strained but honest. The diagnostic side table\n  duplicates facts derivable from named datoms, but it is load-bearing for the\n  lift morphism (B2's `lift(adapt(lower x), side-table)` equation), is outside\n  code identity, and the compliance table declares the strain (\"Binder names\n  and provenance are side data and never executable identity\"). The image-hash\n  exclusion of the side table is the right call.\n- **Loaded-image state / frame env / request emission**: all three declared as\n  strains in the table, with B4's lift requirement preventing an\n  under-approximated `:yin.k/requires` on exported continuations. Honest.\n\n**Table dishonesty check**: one overstatement, the invariant-I row citing\n§7.2's unbuilt request/response machinery (P2-2). No omissions of substance;\nthe table even lists its own strains, which is unusual and to the design's\ncredit.\n\n### 3. Decisions D1–D7\n\n- **D1** (normalizer): consistent with §1 (\"defined and tested in B0, rather\n  than assumed to be an existing repository utility\") and B0. Serves parity,\n  hence invariant I's cross-host agreement. No leftover text.\n- **D2** (drop lossless DAG): consistent with §1, §7.3, and §3's\n  derived-diagnostics language. No phase still references a DAG. Serves\n  derive-don't-persist, indirectly invariant I by keeping one authority.\n- **D3** (descriptor): consistent with §2's raw-Bytes slots, scalar domain,\n  lift morphism, and refusal rule; B1 fixtures match (\"a ratio fixture that is\n  either encoded or refused\"). Serves portability, hence invariant I.\n- **D4** (leak is separate): consistent across §1, §5, B4, B5. But no phase\n  owns the fix (P3-5); the owner may be surprised when park/resume parity is\n  still restricted after B5.\n- **D5** (H is the executable image hash, SCC = one unit, fingerprint never H):\n  consistent with §2 (\"it is not the executable cache key\") and §7.2's SCC\n  ordering. \"Definition unit\" granularity is undefined, but that is B6 scope\n  and does not block B0. Serves invariant I directly.\n- **D6** (linker principles): consistent with §7.2. \"A value-held name\n  environment\" is explicit data inside the linker process, not global state.\n  Serves invariant I, but is future work (see P2-2/P2-3).\n- **D7** (coexistence): consistent with §1's exit criterion, except B3's\n  \"benchmark gate\" wording contradicts it (P2-1).\n\nNothing in D1–D7 blocks B0; the claim \"No owner decision blocks B0\" is\naccurate, since B0 needs only D1 and the frozen corpus.\n\n### 4. Internal consistency and form\n\nForm is clean: no line exceeds 80 columns (verified by regex), no em dashes\n(verified by search), line 3 is exactly \"Status: design; not implemented\",\ntables are ASCII, and there is no model-routing text. Source claims verified\nagainst code: `resolve-name` is public (debruijn.cljc:606); `index-frame`,\n`build-node`, `project-node` are private (436/636/726), matching §3's\n\"not reused\"; `encode-value` exists (887); `vector-operand-table` is public\n(code.cljc:194); `segment-scope` is public while `closure-ranges` and\n`layout-conforms?` are private (completion.cljc:162/132/147), exactly as §3\nstates; `load-vector`, `:code-aliases`, `:yin.code/hash`, `jing/segment-key`,\n`jing/sha256`, `:yin.k/requires` all verified; the projection's UTF-16\nsurrogate guard, `:yin/tail?` drop, binder-name exclusion, and numeric\ncanonicalization match §1–§2's characterizations.\n\nContradictions found: P2-1 (benchmark gate vs informational), P2-3\n(publication \"outside B0 through B6\" vs B6 \"hash-address loading\"), P3-3\n(lift equation vs synthesized names), P3-4 (B6 has no phase box), P3-6 (table\nmisalignment), P3-7 (frame-VM naming). No phase depends on the dropped\nlossless DAG.\n\n### 5. Top three risks to \"share code over dao.stream\"\n\n1. **The pull path is vaporware-adjacent.** After B5, sharing works only when\n   the sender pushes bytes. Hash-addressed retrieval, the request/response\n   convention, publication, and discovery are all deferred (P2-2, P2-3), so\n   the headline capability, \"any yin.vm can obtain the code for H,\" has no\n   owning phase with a schema.\n2. **The common scalar domain is the real portability ceiling.** Ratio,\n   bigint, and char images refuse on hosts lacking those classes, and refusal\n   is terminal with no negotiation or downgrade. Cross-host sharing silently\n   narrows to the least-common-denominator domain, and the design gives no\n   producer-side signal that an image is common-domain-safe before transmission.\n3. **Free names are outside the identity.** The same H can execute, error, or\n   diverge on a receiver whose free-env, store, primitives, or modules differ;\n   there is no load-time resolvability check and no declared outcome for an\n   unbound free name (P3-1). Until B6 dependency closure, \"shares one content\n   address that any yin.vm can run\" holds only under an unstated\n   same-name-environment assumption.\n\n---\n\n## CHECKED AND FOUND CLEAN\n\n- Form: 80-column limit, no em dashes, status line, ASCII tables, no\n  model-routing text.\n- All §2/§3/§7.2 source claims against debruijn.cljc, code.cljc,\n  completion.cljc, content.cljc, semantic.cljc, engine.cljc, jing.cljc, and\n  yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md (public/private visibility, helper names,\n  attribute names, UTF-16 guard, tail-flag and binder-name handling).\n- datom.world compliance: no hidden global state, no callbacks, no shared\n  mutable state, no implicit control flow, no layer collapse, no assumed\n  graphs, host boundary = stream boundary; the three declared strains\n  (frame env, loaded-image state, diagnostics) are genuine strains, honestly\n  labeled, with B4's lift requirement closing the `:yin.k/requires` hole.\n- D1–D7 alignment between §8, the architect's rulings\n  (collab/1790017487131, including the D4 MODIFY), and the body text.\n- B0 startability: D1 plus the frozen parity corpus is sufficient; no deferred\n  item gates it.","stopReason":"end_turn","turnCount":10,"usage":{"inputTokens":329777,"outputTokens":12920,"cacheReadTokens":283648,"cacheWriteTokens":0},"systemPromptTokens":null,"nextState":{"sessionId":"10e55420-8cfe-45ac-b2b3-866bd4507648","messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created-GMT: 2026-09-21 19:08:29 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-09-22 02:08:29 +07 (Indochina Time)\nCoding-Agent: cmd (qwen/qwen3.8-max) | deepseek (see the launch line; this brief is shared by two independent reviewers)\nSession-ID: pending (provider-generated) for cmd; caller-generated for deepseek (see stdout log name)\n# Task: debruijn-vm-decisions — independent review of the design's decisions against two invariants\nRole: Adversarial / Routine Review (VM Runtime, Architecture)\nImplementers:\n- Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max (Qwen family, via cmd) and deepseek-v4-pro (DeepSeek family) | Assigned: 2026-09-22 02:08:29 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: the design was authored by gpt-5.6-sol and reviewed by the Claude family (opus-5 four passes, fable-5-1 once); the owner authorized these two other families (qwen3.8-max chosen over laguna-s-2.1) for an independent look at the final decisions\n\nRead-only. Work in /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld. You can read files and run\nread-only git commands; do not edit or create any file, and do not run tests.\nProduce the complete review now as your final response; do not wait for approval\nand do not promise a verdict.\n\n## What you are reviewing\n\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md (untracked, ~500 lines): a design for a yin.vm that\nexecutes a name-resolved de Bruijn instruction image derived from the NAMED AST\ndatoms, coexisting with the current semantic VM, so that code can be shared over\n`dao.stream`. It was just updated to record decisions D1-D7 (the architect's\nruling and rationale are in\ncollab/1790017487131-architect-debruijn-vm-decisions.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md).\n\nThe OWNER'S INVARIANT, verbatim: \"I want de Bruijn projection so that a yin.vm can\neasily share code over dao.stream linker.\" Reading adopted by the design: the LOSSY\nprojection (docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md, merged) is the alpha-\nequivalence identity layer; what a yin.vm executes and shares is an EXECUTABLE IMAGE\nderived from the named datoms with the same de Bruijn resolution, whose content\nidentity is alpha-invariant for binders (binder names outside the hash), so\nequivalent programs share one content address that any yin.vm can obtain, verify\nand run through the stream linker (a process between streams; local or remote is\njust a stream).\n\nThe SECOND SET of invariants: docs/design/datom.world.md (axioms and\nnon-negotiable invariants: no hidden global state, no implicit control flow, no\ncallbacks, no shared mutable state, do not collapse interpretation and execution,\nno assumed graphs; \"derive, don't persist\"; a host boundary is a stream boundary;\nan adapter takes no function to invoke).\n\n## Read (in this order)\n\n1. docs/design/datom.world.md\n2. docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md (in full)\n3. collab/1790017487131-architect-debruijn-vm-decisions.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md\n4. Only if you need to check a claim: docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md,\n   docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md sections 1-2, src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc,\n   src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc, src/cljc/yin/vm/content.cljc.\n\n## Answer (be concrete; quote the sentence you are judging)\n\n1. **Invariant I.** Does the design, as decided, actually let a yin.vm easily share\n   code over the dao.stream linker? Walk one concrete scenario end to end: host A\n   lowers a program, publishes the image, host B (a different runtime, e.g. Dart)\n   receives it over a dao.stream, verifies it against its hash, and runs it. Say\n   what happens at each step, which design mechanism covers it, and where the\n   chain has a gap or an unstated assumption (for example: what B does if the image\n   uses a scalar class B lacks; how B obtains the image when it only knows H; what\n   the free names inside the image resolve to on B).\n2. **datom.world invariants.** For each non-negotiable invariant and \"derive, don't\n   persist\", find any sentence or mechanism in the design that violates or strains\n   it (loaded-image state, the frame environment, the diagnostic side table, the\n   name environment, request emission, image caching). Check the design's own\n   compliance table for dishonesty or omissions.\n3. **The decisions D1-D7.** For each, is it consistent with the rest of the\n   document (no leftover text contradicting it), and does it serve invariant I?\n   Flag any decision that is under-specified enough to block B0, or that will\n   surprise the owner later.\n4. **Internal consistency and form.** Contradictions between sections, phases B0-B5\n   that depend on something dropped, stale text, and form (80 columns, no em\n   dashes, ASCII tables, status \"design; not implemented\", no model-routing text).\n5. **Top three risks** to the goal \"share code over dao.stream\".\n\n## Deliverable\n\nFinal response beginning exactly with:\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\nCoding-Agent: <cmd|deepseek>\nSession-ID: <your session id if visible, else pending>\nthen a verdict: SOUND, SOUND WITH CHANGES, or NOT SOUND; findings as P1 (the design\nwould fail the owner's invariant or a datom.world invariant), P2 (a significant\ngap), P3 (minor), each with the section, the quoted sentence, a concrete failing\nscenario, and the smallest fix; the answers to items 1-5; and what you checked and\nfound clean. Findings only; edit no file."}],"meta":{"source":"user","createdAt":1790017810774,"messageId":"484b0e33-127a-4225-b2a7-a6f56f2ae82a"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a review task. I'm in plan mode, but the task is read-only in any case, and asks for the review as the final answer. In plan mode, I'm supposed to write a plan... but this task is explicitly a review artifact. The instructions state that the review should be produced as the final answer. Plan mode restricts writes, but reading is fine. Rather than writing a plan file, I should just conduct the review and produce an answer — the artifact is the answer itself.\n\nFirst, let me read the necessary documents. I'll start by reading docs/design/datom.world.md, docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md, and the findings from collab.\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I'll conduct this review. Let me read the required documents in order.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_41df93987cd34fe2bff2ded5","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/datom.world.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_540bb328d8d9407b8ad4ca90","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1790017822154,"messageId":"da5315a4-d95f-458f-a137-4b67ffc87495"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_41df93987cd34fe2bff2ded5","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 178 lines\n1: ---\n2: description: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n3: ---\n4: \n5: # DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n6: \n7: ## Core Philosophy\n8: \n9: Everything is data.\n10: Interpretation > abstraction.\n11: Explicit causality > implicit assumptions.\n12: Restrictions are a feature.\n13: \n14: ### Foundational Axioms\n15: \n16: 1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n17: 2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n18: 3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n19: 4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n20: \n21: ## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n22: \n23: - Do not introduce hidden global state.\n24: - Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n25: - Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n26: - Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n27: - Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n28: - Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n29: \n30: ## Tuples and Datoms\n31: \n32: Tuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\n33: A datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\n34: Source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n35: \n36: See [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n37: \n38: ## Streams\n39: \n40: All IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\n41: No direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\n42: Side effects must appear as stream emissions.\n43: Streams are values that can be sent through streams.\n44: Streams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n45: \n46: A stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\n47: of interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\n48: semantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\n49: stream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\n50: the very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\n51: answers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\n52: exists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\n53: the other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\n54: and the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\n55: becomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\n56: the stream's own.\n57: \n58: Functions can take anything and return anything.\n59: Agents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n60: \n61: See [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\n62: See [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n63: \n64: ### Host Boundaries\n65: \n66: A host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\n67: sanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\n68: exhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n69: \n70: **Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\n71: happen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\n72: appends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\n73: identity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\n74: no implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\n75: correct outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n76: \n77: **Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\n78: a timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\n79: nothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n80: Constructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\n81: to data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n82: \n83: **Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\n84: adapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\n85: timers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\n86: what they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\n87: the append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\n88: to the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\n89: deposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\n90: may close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\n91: not an upward callback.\n92: \n93: Host types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\n94: place holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\n95: transport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\n96: additive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\n97: event mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\n98: for facts it owns.\n99: \n100: Which stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\n101: adapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\n102: transport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\n103: the layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\n104: constructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n105: \n106: A transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\n107: none of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\n108: happen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\n109: event. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n110: (axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\n111: does not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\n112: not yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\n113: interpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n114: \n115: Two things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\n116: call isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\n117: appears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\n118: callback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\n119: that callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\n120: directions wearing the shape of an event.\n121: \n122: ## Architectural Commitments\n123: \n124: - **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n125: - **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n126: - **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n127: - **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n128: \n129: ## Design Principles\n130: \n131: Code quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\n132: Stigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n133: \n134: **Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\n135: canonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\n136: shape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n137: `dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\n138: query or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\n139: consumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\n140: retired `:global` tag in\n141: [`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\n142: case study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n143: \n144: See [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\n145: See [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n146: \n147: ## Code Style\n148: \n149: - Prefer simple data over rich types.\n150: - Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n151: - Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n152: - Avoid cleverness.\n153: - Name things after what they do to streams.\n154: \n155: ## Development Workflow\n156: \n157: - Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n158: - Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n159: - Do not optimize prematurely.\n160: - When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n161: - Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n162: \n163: ### Problem Decomposition\n164: \n165: If a problem appears complex:\n166: 1. Decompose it into streams.\n167: 2. Identify invariants.\n168: 3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n169: 4. Make causality explicit.\n170: 5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n171: \n172: ## Operational & Specialized Guides\n173: \n174: - **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n175: - **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n176: - **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n177: - **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n178: - **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/file-format.md`](../agents/file-format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications."}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_540bb328d8d9407b8ad4ca90","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 541 lines\n1: # yin.vm de Bruijn VM\n2: \n3: Status: design; not implemented\n4: \n5: This document specifies a second executable path for `yin.vm`. The existing\n6: path lowers named Universal AST datoms to `:yin.code/*` and executes that\n7: image. This design defines a sibling VM for a derived linear image. No second\n8: lossless source view is built. The projection remains a separate identity and\n9: deduplication artifact.\n10: \n11: ## 1. Architecture and invariants\n12: \n13: Design invariant I: a yin.vm can share executable code over a `dao.stream`\n14: linker. The linker may be local or remote, but its boundary is always the\n15: stream.\n16: \n17: Three artifacts are associated with one named AST. The named datoms are the\n18: bijective source representation. The projection is the alpha-canonical,\n19: lossy identity representation. The executable de Bruijn image is a third,\n20: executable, derived representation and is not itself invertible.\n21: \n22: Architecture A means lowering from projected records. Architecture B, the\n23: recommended architecture, lowers from named datoms and is not the same as A.\n24: Lowering projected records cannot promise named execution semantics because\n25: projection deliberately canonicalizes scalar spelling, preserves free names\n26: only in canonical form, removes binder names and provenance, and drops\n27: front-end `:yin/tail?`. Restricting the input to already-canonical programs,\n28: weakening continuation guarantees, and abandoning the fingerprint as a cache\n29: key would make that path sound but would make it a poor executable artifact.\n30: \n31: Named datoms remain the lossless truth. Binder names and provenance are kept\n32: in the diagnostic side table used by the executable image, while lexical\n33: positions are derived by `resolve-name`; no second lossless fact structure is\n34: persisted. This follows the datom.world rule \"derive, don't persist\".\n35: \n36: The executable VM promises equivalence with the named semantic VM only after\n37: normalization. Results, effects, streams, cursors, stores, terminal and\n38: blocked outcomes, and errors must agree. Error comparison uses the message;\n39: ex-data is compared after recursively applying the value normalizer to every\n40: ex-data value. Closures compare as `{:type :closure :arity n}` only;\n41: continuation and parked values compare by type only; stream and cursor\n42: references compare by identity; host handles and telemetry are excluded.\n43: Behavioural application tests cover closure contents. This normalizer is\n44: defined and tested in B0, rather than assumed to be an existing repository\n45: utility.\n46: \n47: The free environment is the VM's initial environment value, fixed for one VM\n48: instance. It is not the mutable callee `:env` register left by an earlier\n49: named `run`. The named VM's environment leak is a separate defect and is not\n50: silently included in this contract. B5 uses fresh VM instances for ordinary\n51: parity. Park/resume fixtures that cross program boundaries are restricted to\n52: free names supplied by the initial environment or store until the named-VM\n53: leak is fixed; otherwise B5 records a named-path refusal.\n54: \n55: The following are not promised to be identical: binder spelling in the\n56: projected fingerprint, instruction program counters, temporary ids, allocation\n57: identity, stack-trace text, source-map formatting, or interchangeability of\n58: continuations between the two VMs. Named datoms remain available for complete\n59: decoding and debugging; the executable image is not an inverse encoding.\n60: \n61: The VM obeys the datom.world invariants: state is explicit data, execution is\n62: an interpreter above streams, callbacks are never retained, effects are\n63: stream-visible, and the existing AST, projection, lease, waitset, and named VM\n64: contracts are not changed.\n65: \n66: The following table records compliance with design invariant I and the\n67: datom.world principles.\n68: \n69:     +--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+\n70:     | Requirement              | Mechanism                                  |\n71:     +--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+\n72:     | I: share executable code | Canonical image hash, verified loading,    |\n73:     | over dao.stream           | stream request and response (§2, §7.2).   |\n74:     | Everything is a stream   | Effects, transfer, linking and failure    |\n75:     |                          | are stream outcomes (§4, §7.2).           |\n76:     | Everything is a          | Parked machine state is explicit data and |\n77:     | continuation             | resumes through stream-carried events     |\n78:     |                          | (§4, §7.2).                               |\n79:     | No hidden global state   | VM state, free-env and registries are     |\n80:     |                          | explicit values (§4).                     |\n81:     | No implicit control flow | Steps classify explicit data; missing     |\n82:     |                          | code parks and emits a request (§7.2).    |\n83:     | No callbacks             | No loader or callback is invoked; events  |\n84:     |                          | cross stream boundaries (§7.2).           |\n85:     | No shared mutable state  | Frames, stores and continuations are      |\n86:     |                          | persistent transition data (§4).          |\n87:     | No layer collapse       | Projection, lowering, VM and linker are   |\n88:     |                          | separate interpreters (§1, §7.2).         |\n89:     | No assumed graphs       | Image refs and body ranges are validated  |\n90:     |                          | before execution (§3).                    |\n91:     | Derive, do not persist   | Named datoms stay authoritative; lexical  |\n92:     |                          | positions are derived (§1).               |\n93:     | Interpretation semantics| Named and de Bruijn VMs are compared by  |\n94:     |                          | the explicit normalizer (§1, B0).         |\n95:     | Host boundaries         | Cross-host common domain is tested;       |\n96:     |                          | unsupported classes refuse (§2, B1).      |\n97:     | Strained: frame env     | Frames require a lift for completion and  |\n98:     |                          | exported continuations (§4, B4).         |\n99:     | Strained: image state   | Loaded images are explicit values; the   |\n100:     |                          | linker must supply missing-code events.   |\n101:     | Strained: diagnostics   | Binder names and provenance are side data |\n102:     |                          | and never executable identity (§2, §3).   |\n103:     +--------------------------+--------------------------------------------+\n104: \n105: ### Benefit and exit criterion\n106: \n107: The primary benefit is network-shareable, alpha-invariant, content-addressed\n108: executable code over `dao.stream`. Existing named code is already content\n109: addressed by `yin.vm.content`; this adds binder-alpha-invariant image identity\n110: and portable bytes for the common scalar domain. Free names still resolve by\n111: name until the stream linker in §7.2 supplies dependency closure. Physical Jing\n112: addresses remain print-based until the DaoJing CBOR work lands; the image hash\n113: uses its own canonical encoding and is independent of that address.\n114: \n115: After B3, the semantic VM section 8 harness reports throughput, allocation,\n116: image size, and load time on identical pure-program corpora. The report is\n117: informational, not an acceptance condition. Retiring the semantic VM would\n118: require its own design because existing consumers depend on it; the owner may\n119: instead make the frame VM the default while retaining the semantic VM.\n120: \n121: ## 2. Instruction dimension and code identity\n122: \n123: The executable image uses a new `:yin.debruijn.code/*` dimension. The\n124: implementation defines and exports its descriptor; persistent publication and\n125: discovery are outside B0 through B6. Existing `:yin.code/*` semantics are\n126: unchanged. The new descriptor derives its opcode table as data from the\n127: existing `yin.vm.code/vector-operand-table`; only lexical addressing differs:\n128: `:var` becomes `:load-bound` or `:load-free`, and `:closure` carries\n129: arity and body references rather than parameter symbols. The stack operations,\n130: `:const`, `:push`, `:halt`, `:branch-false`, stream operations, store\n131: operations, park, resume, gensym, and FFI retain their existing opcode shapes.\n132: Its descriptor declares arity, ordered slots, canonical encoding, and one lift\n133: morphism from `:yin.debruijn.code/*` to `:yin.code/*`; the lift uses diagnostic\n134: binder names or synthesized names. Exact-spelling slots are typed raw `Bytes`,\n135: so no-NFC behavior is a slot rule, not an exception to the dimension contract.\n136: \n137: The new code namespace copies its small framing and length-prefix helpers and\n138: defines its own scalar tag table; it does not reach private projection vars.\n139: It uses `jing/sha256` and a separate executable scalar encoding: distinct tags\n140: represent nil, booleans, long, double, ratio, bigint, char, string, keyword,\n141: symbol, vectors, lists, maps, sets, and other explicitly supported values.\n142: Map and set entries are ordered by encoded bytes; vectors and lists are\n143: distinct classes. Numeric bits and string/name UTF-8 bytes are used exactly as\n144: supplied; no NFC or integral-double folding occurs in hashed bytes.\n145: Unpaired UTF-16 surrogates are refused with `:unsupported-value`, as they are\n146: by the projection's UTF-16 guard. Values outside the declared executable\n147: domain are refused with a qualified `unsupported-value` diagnostic, not\n148: silently canonicalized. This is deliberately distinct\n149: from `yin.vm.debruijn/encode-value`.\n150: \n151: Binder names and provenance are carried in a diagnostic side table indexed by\n152: instruction slot and are outside the image hash. Exact scalar bytes are in\n153: the `:const` operands and are included in the image hash; free names remain\n154: hashed `:load-free` operands.\n155: \n156: Host classification is explicit. On CLJS, a JavaScript number is encoded as a\n157: long when it is a safe integer and as a double otherwise; CLJS cannot\n158: distinguish source spellings that already read as the same number. Characters\n159: are host characters where available and strings otherwise. Ratios and host\n160: bigints are JVM or Dart classes unless a host provides an equivalent. Cross-\n161: host byte identity is required only for values present on all three hosts.\n162: B1 tests for `1` versus `1.0`, ratios, and chars are JVM/Dart tests; shared\n163: cross-host fixtures cover the common scalar domain.\n164: \n165: The image records the scalar classes it uses. A receiving host that lacks a\n166: class refuses the image with a qualified `unsupported-value` outcome before\n167: execution; this is a host-boundary outcome, not a stream gap.\n168: \n169: The code-image identity is the hash of the canonical positional instruction\n170: vector: pc-indexed tuples, refs resolved to pcs, no header, descriptor hash,\n171: and exact scalar bytes. This is the same canonical\n172: form used by `load-image` and `jing/segment-key`. Provenance is a diagnostic\n173: side table indexed by pc and is outside the hash. The projection fingerprint\n174: may be recorded as metadata and used to find alpha-equivalent candidates, but\n175: it is not the executable cache key. Two programs with equal projection\n176: fingerprints may therefore have different executable images, for example\n177: because their literal spelling or tail flags differ.\n178: No cache is specified by B0 through B5; any later cache is an explicit value\n179: keyed by the canonical image hash.\n180: \n181: ## 3. Lowering and scope\n182: \n183: The lowerer receives complete `:yin/*` named datoms plus the projected\n184: fingerprint as optional metadata. It reuses only the public\n185: `yin.vm.debruijn/resolve-name` helper. That helper compares names exactly,\n186: including rightmost-wins duplicate parameters, and does not canonicalize\n187: values. Projection-only helpers such as `index-frame`, `build-node`, and\n188: `project-node` are not reused. Unexpanded-macro validation remains the named\n189: front end's responsibility.\n190: \n191: The walk is deterministic and left to right. Lambda bodies are out of line,\n192: applications evaluate operator then operands, and `if` evaluates one arm.\n193: The lowerer copies the front end's `:yin/tail?` exactly as the named linearizer\n194: does. It does not infer tail position from syntax. Only `:application` has a\n195: tail operand in the existing instruction table; `:dao.stream.apply/call`\n196: lowers to `:ffi-call` and has no tail operand.\n197: \n198: The image inherits occurrence expansion, fresh lambda labels, and body layout\n199: from `yin.vm.linearize/lower`; B2 does not repeat that traversal. Any adapter\n200: memo is keyed by the emitted body and pc context. If a future source-level\n201: memo is introduced, its key must include node identity, the full vector of\n202: parameter-name vectors, and tail context. Frame arities remain separate scope\n203: validation data and cannot determine name resolution.\n204: \n205: The executable image may carry a debug node hash and named source reference.\n206: Those fields are always diagnostic metadata outside code identity. They replace\n207: no named source map.\n208: \n209: Scope validation is mandatory in both places where an image can enter:\n210: \n211: 1. B2 validates nonnegative depth and position against the frame-arity vector.\n212: 2. B1's image validator repeats the check by walking each body's declared\n213:    arity and enclosing-body chain. A shape-valid hand-built image with\n214:    `[:load-bound [5 0]]` is rejected before execution.\n215: \n216: The projected reader's existing tuple-shape check remains useful, but it is\n217: not a scope check.\n218: \n219: ## 4. VM state and execution\n220: \n221: The sibling VM uses explicit state:\n222: \n223:     {:segment code-image\n224:      :pc pc\n225:      :frames [frame ...]\n226:      :free-env initial-name-map\n227:      :stack operand-vector\n228:      :continuation continuation-data\n229:      :store store\n230:      :status status}\n231: \n232: Frames are positional vectors ordered outermost to innermost; frame zero for a\n233: bound reference is the innermost frame, read from the end of the vector. A\n234: closure captures the persistent frame stack and its body reference. Calls use\n235: the positional equivalent of `bind-params`: `(vec (take arity (concat args\n236: (repeat nil))))`. Missing arguments are nil-filled and extras are dropped.\n237: The existing named `bind-params` remains name-keyed and unchanged.\n238: \n239: `:load-free` uses the existing `resolve-var` order with `free-env`, store,\n240: primitives, and module registry. Positional locals never fall through to a\n241: store key. `:macro?` is retained only in named datoms; runtime closure\n242: application ignores it exactly as the named runtime does after macro expansion.\n243: \n244: The VM is a new `yin.vm.debruijn-vm` namespace. It may implement existing VM\n245: protocols without adding methods, and it may reuse data-only engine helpers\n246: for name resolution, primitive descriptions, stream descriptors, store\n247: operations, gensym, and continuation records. It reimplements any helper\n248: that serializes the named register layout, including private response wait\n249: entries and named telemetry snapshots. No existing protocol or VM semantics\n250: change.\n251: \n252: It does not implement `vm/IVMState/environment` until a frame-to-named lift is\n253: defined. If that method is later implemented, it returns the lifted named\n254: environment, not the positional frame vector.\n255: \n256: Park and resume carry the frame stack, free environment, operand stack, code\n257: identity, continuation chain, store view, parked records, gensym counter,\n258: primitives, modules, code aliases, and the semantic VM's other non-environment\n259: registers as explicit data. B4 supplies a frame-aware completion adapter or a\n260: lift through the descriptor morphism before exporting a continuation. UCF\n261: remains proposed and deferred, so heterogeneous continuation transport is not\n262: yet promised.\n263: \n264: ## 5. Effects and equivalence boundaries\n265: \n266: The executable path covers the existing literal, variable, lambda,\n267: application, `if`, stream, store, gensym, continuation, park, resume, and FFI\n268: operations. Macro expansion remains upstream; unexpanded macro diagnostics\n269: are not runtime execution.\n270: \n271: Equivalence excludes the named VM's stale-register leak. A named VM instance\n272: used for B5 must begin with a fixed initial environment, or the test records a\n273: named-path defect and does not claim parity. It also excludes representation\n274: details that the normalizer intentionally erases. It does not exclude exact\n275: literal values, spelling-sensitive store keys, front-end tail choices, stream\n276: outcomes, or error classifications.\n277: \n278: ## 6. Implementation phases\n279: \n280: Each phase has a file box, a must-not-change list, completion criteria, and\n281: JVM, Node/CLJS, ClojureDart, kondo, and cljstyle verification.\n282: \n283: ### B0: contract and normalizer\n284: \n285:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_contract_test.cljc\n286:     Existing edits: none\n287:     Must not change: AST, emitter, projection, named VM, code dimension\n288: \n289: Freeze the result/error normalizer and actual parity corpus:\n290: `parity-test` plus the every-tag corpora in `content_test` and\n291: `completion_test`. Completion requires normalized closure, continuation, error,\n292: stream, cursor, and store comparisons, plus duplicate-parameter and all-node\n293: fixtures.\n294: \n295: ### B1: executable dimension and validator\n296: \n297:     New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc\n298:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc\n299:     Existing edits: none\n300:     Must not change: yin.vm.code, :yin.code/*, projection encoder\n301: \n302: Define and export the descriptor, mirrored opcode table, exact executable\n303: scalar encoder, code-image hash, and validator. Completion includes malformed\n304: rows,\n305: out-of-range bound operands, exact spelling preservation, distinct hashes for\n306: composed and decomposed e-acute, a ratio fixture that is either encoded or\n307: refused with `:unsupported-value`, distinct hashes for `1`/`1.0`, ratios, and\n308: chars on JVM and Dart, and\n309: identical bytes across hosts for the common scalar domain.\n310: \n311: ### B2: named-datom lowerer adapter\n312: \n313:     New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc\n314:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize_test.cljc\n315:     Existing edits: none\n316:     Must not change: yin.vm.linearize and named lowering semantics\n317: \n318: Run the existing `yin.vm.linearize/lower` on the named datoms, then rewrite\n319: each `:var` operand and replace each `:closure` parameter vector with its\n320: arity. Scope reconstruction is a pure function of the image, never of\n321: `:yin.code/source`: each body range is `[entry, first :return]`, its parent is\n322: the body containing the `:closure`, and the name stack for `resolve-name` is\n323: built innermost-first. This is opposite to the runtime frame vector's\n324: outermost-to-innermost order and the conversion is explicit. `segment-scope`\n325: is the public precedent; `closure-ranges` and `layout-conforms?` are private\n326: helpers whose rules are reproduced or exposed in the new namespace. A failed\n327: `layout-conforms?` check is a validation defect. Completion requires\n328: deterministic output, every node and opcode, lexical validation, a structural\n329: opcode-by-opcode comparison with `lower` differing only at variable and\n330: closure operands, image encode/validate/load round trips, and\n331: `lift(adapt(lower x), side-table) = canonical-vector(lower x)`. The lift is a\n332: function of the image plus its diagnostic side table. With synthesized names,\n333: the lifted result is alpha-equivalent to `lower x`, including\n334: `(fn [x x] x)`.\n335: \n336: ### B3: frame VM kernel\n337: \n338:     New: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc\n339:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_test.cljc\n340:     Existing edits: none\n341:     Must not change: semantic VM, engine, IVM protocols, named environment\n342: \n343: Implement frames, closures, loads, calls, returns, branches, literals, and\n344: store operations. Completion requires pure-program parity using B0's\n345: normalizer and fresh initial environments, plus the section 1 benchmark gate\n346: for throughput, allocation, image size, and load time.\n347: \n348: ### B4: effects and continuations\n349: \n350:     Existing source: src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_vm.cljc\n351:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_effects_test.cljc\n352:     Existing edits: none\n353:     Must not change: dao.stream protocols, lease, waitset, named effect rules\n354: \n355: Implement stream operations, primitives, FFI, gensym, current-continuation,\n356: park, and resume. Completion requires parity for values, errors, effects,\n357: stores, stream outcomes, and blocked states. Cross-program park/resume tests\n358: must use only initial-environment or store-resolved free names until the\n359: named-VM environment leak is fixed. Before a continuation is exported, B4\n360: must either lift frames through the descriptor morphism or run the frame-aware\n361: completion adapter so `:yin.k/requires` is not under-approximated.\n362: \n363: ### B5: differential integration\n364: \n365:     New: test/yin/vm/debruijn_vm_parity_test.cljc\n366:     Existing edits: none\n367:     Must not change: named storage and the existing linearizer pipeline\n368: \n369: Compare `ast->datoms` -> projection metadata plus B2 -> de Bruijn VM against\n370: `ast->datoms` -> `linearize/lower` -> semantic VM. Use the actual parity and\n371: content/completion corpora, not helper-only tests as execution fixtures.\n372: Completion requires alpha-equivalent programs to share projection identity\n373: and binder-renamed programs to produce the same image hash on every host lane\n374: for the common scalar domain. Programs differing in exact scalar spelling,\n375: front-end tail flags, or free names must produce different image hashes. An\n376: image sent over a `dao.stream` from one host lane must load, validate, and\n377: execute on another with the same normalized result as local execution. All\n378: three host lanes must agree under the normalizer.\n379: \n380: ## 7. Test matrix and non-goals\n381: \n382: The matrix includes exact scalar spelling, names, tail flags,\n383: provenance, scope, duplicate parameters, nil fill, extras dropped, control\n384: flow, streams, effects, continuations, macros, malformed images, and all\n385: existing parity/content/completion execution fixtures. It also includes\n386: alpha-equivalent binder renames with equal image hashes, exact-spelling and\n387: tail-flag differences with distinct hashes, free-name changes, stream transfer,\n388: and cross-host code-image bytes.\n389: \n390: Non-goals are equality saturation, a new JIT, global distribution, dependency\n391: hash linking before B6, and any change to the projection or its fingerprint.\n392: \n393: ### 7.1 Prior art: Unison's runtime\n394: \n395: Unison's published runtime documentation describes let-rec minimization,\n396: lambda lifting, ANF, an IR with De Bruijn indices and stack positions, and\n397: decompilation. The supplied source listing is reported to contain runtime\n398: components including ANF, MCode, Machine, Serialize, Decompile, and\n399: Canonicalizer. These facts support architecture B as an analogy:\n400: the executable form is derived from a full-information term and uses explicit\n401: stack positions, rather than being lowered from a lossy identity projection.\n402: They do not establish that Unison executes its exact stored identity form.\n403: \n404: The named linearizer already supplies a deterministic stack-oriented image.\n405: Lambda lifting, ANF, and let-rec minimization are therefore not adopted by\n406: this design: lambda lifting would change closure capture into extra ordinary\n407: parameters, and the resulting calling convention is a separate optimization\n408: contract. They remain possible upstream AST-to-AST stream stages as described\n409: below. Unison's decompilation\n410: to displayable terms is not assumed here to preserve exact source datoms, tail\n411: flags, tempids, or provenance. This design infers no stronger decompilation\n412: property from those sources.\n413: \n414: In this project, lambda lifting or ANF may be separate AST-to-AST stream\n415: stages upstream of both lowerers. Such a stage reads and writes named\n416: `:yin/*` datoms, is optional, and changes arity. Synthesized parameters need\n417: name-table entries. It must write `:yin/tail?` on every node it creates,\n418: because both lowerers copy that flag and infer nothing. Lifted and unlifted\n419: programs must be compared under an explicit arity caveat, and the B0\n420: normalizer applies to whichever named AST the lowerer receives. This VM\n421: neither depends on nor forbids those stages.\n422: \n423: The verified identity documentation is:\n424: \n425:     https://www.unison-lang.org/docs/the-big-idea/\n426:     https://unison-lang.org/whats-new/writeup-of-our-first-unison-meetup\n427: \n428: The verified runtime documentation and source directory are:\n429: \n430:     https://raw.githubusercontent.com/unisonweb/unison/trunk/\n431:       unison-runtime/src/Unison/Runtime/docs.markdown\n432:     https://github.com/unisonweb/unison/tree/trunk/\n433:       unison-runtime/src/Unison/Runtime\n434: \n435: UNVERIFIED here: evaluation as a documented pipeline stage, the detailed\n436: component listing, whether the runtime input is the stored identity artifact,\n437: reference resolution, MCode and Machine semantics, and whether\n438: `docs.markdown` is current.\n439: \n440: ### 7.2 Future reference-by-hash linking as a stream topology\n441: \n442: Reference-by-hash is a later epic, not B-phase VM machinery. The linker is a\n443: process between streams: it consumes executable images and a\n444: value-held name environment, uses the projection fingerprint only as a lookup\n445: index, emits verified definition requests, consumes definition datoms,\n446: and emits a linked image or an explicit refusal. This follows the axiom that\n447: all IO and data flow through append-only streams. No global mutable registry\n448: is introduced, and no direct function-to-function call crosses the boundary.\n449: The boundary is `dao.stream`; local and remote resolution are the same\n450: transport-neutral mechanism, and their physical placement is not a linker\n451: decision.\n452: \n453: `yin.vm.content` verifies content-addressed rows and vectors. `load-vector`\n454: records the `jing/segment-key` in `:code-aliases`; `:yin.code/hash` is the\n455: batch attribute checked when loading an image. `yin.vm.completion` computes\n456: conservative named-VM closure facts and `:yin.k/requires`, including reachable\n457: segments, stores, parked records, primitives, and modules. None rewrites a\n458: free name to a definition hash, and the completion adapter is not yet a\n459: de Bruijn adapter.\n460: \n461: A future linked dimension may contain `:call-hash H`. If `H` is unavailable,\n462: the linked interpreter must park its explicit continuation and emit a request\n463: record on a request stream. A definition-arrival consumer reads datoms from a\n464: response stream, verifies their content address through `yin.vm.content`,\n465: loads them, and resumes the parked continuation. The request token, parked\n466: continuation, and response are data; no callback is retained. Gaps, timeouts,\n467: and permanent absence remain explicit stream outcomes. The request convention,\n468: arrival consumer, and name-environment stream are new.\n469: \n470: Cycles are decided here as a linker principle: mutually recursive definitions\n471: form one strongly connected component, whose members are ordered by canonical\n472: image hash before the component hash is computed. Authority\n473: also remains a decision: a name environment needs a declared ledger, trust,\n474: and provenance. Verification is supplied by Jing and the loader, while the\n475: linked consumer must reject absent or mismatched content before execution.\n476: Streams provide failure events but do not choose retry or terminal policy.\n477: \n478: This does not require a semantic change to B0 through B5. B3 already makes\n479: loading and continuation state explicit, and B4 carries park and resume as\n480: data transitions. The VM accepts loaded images only as values in its state and\n481: never invokes a loader; absence is a park plus a request emission. B6 is the\n482: additive phase for request and response streams, the\n483: linked dimension, `:call-hash`, cycle handling, and failure policy.\n484: \n485: Completion requires hash-address loading, cycle-safe manifests, explicit\n486: authority, gap and timeout outcomes, malformed or missing-address diagnostics,\n487: and differential tests showing named and linked images agree where the name\n488: environment is fixed. It must not alter the projection fingerprint. It does\n489: not establish global distribution or any property of the Unison runtime,\n490: which is UNVERIFIED here.\n491: \n492: ### 7.3 Lossless source view non-goal\n493: \n494: The VM does not build a second lossless record DAG. Named datoms remain the\n495: lossless source, and lexical positions, binder diagnostics, and provenance are\n496: derived into the executable image as needed. A separate inverse view would\n497: require its own design and is outside B0 through B6.\n498: \n499: ## 8. Risks and owner decisions\n500: \n501: Risks include divergence from front-end tail flags, exact scalar spelling,\n502: named-VM environment leakage, malformed bound operands, source-map loss, and\n503: UCF's deferred transport contract.\n504: \n505: DECIDED:\n506: \n507: 1. D1 normalizer: adopt recursive ex-data normalization, closure type and\n508:    arity comparison, continuation and parked type comparison, stream/cursor\n509:    ids, host-handle-free stores, and telemetry exclusion. This serves\n510:    interpretation parity without inventing hidden state.\n511: 2. D2 lossless DAG: drop it from B0 through B5 and make it a non-goal. Named\n512:    datoms remain authoritative; lexical positions and diagnostics are derived.\n513:    This serves \"derive, don't persist\" and removes redundant authority.\n514: 3. D3 descriptor: adopt the arity, slots, raw-Bytes encoding, scalar domain,\n515:    and lift to `:yin.code/*`. Unsupported host classes refuse before running.\n516: 4. D4 environment leak: treat it as a separate named-VM defect outside these\n517:    phases; retain the stated fixture restriction until fixed.\n518: 5. D5 hash identity: `:call-hash H` names the canonical executable image of a\n519:    definition unit; a recursive component is one hashed unit. The projection\n520:    fingerprint is never H.\n521: 6. D6 linker principles: the name environment is a value or stream, trust and\n522:    provenance are composition policy, SCCs hash as units with canonical member\n523:    ordering, and retry, timeout, and absence are stream events. The boundary\n524:    is always `dao.stream`, whether local or remote.\n525: 7. D7 end state: both VMs coexist. B3 reports benchmark numbers only; the\n526:    semantic VM is never retired without its own design.\n527: \n528: DEFERRED:\n529: \n530: - B6 request schema, contract stamps, SCC manifest mechanics, and the exact\n531:   retry, timeout, and permanent-absence event vocabulary.\n532: - Optional source-to-projection lineage for diagnostics.\n533: \n534: No owner decision blocks B0. B0 starts with D1 and the frozen parity corpus.\n535: \n536: ## 9. What this design must not change\n537: \n538: The AST schema, AST walker, named emitter, named linearizer, semantic VM,\n539: existing `:yin.code/*` dimension, `dao.stream` protocols, lease layer, waitset,\n540: named storage, projection algorithm, and projection fingerprint remain\n541: unchanged. The executable encoding and VM are additional artifacts."}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"e3d3a1e9-e8c9-464b-84bd-b2ec51055e47"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, I'll read the investigation results of the decisions made by the architect.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_5c411afd913d49e7902f03ef","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790017487131-architect-debruijn-vm-decisions.gpt-5.6-sol.findings.md"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1790017828519,"messageId":"4aa4556e-27d7-4ebf-80b3-024bee605d4e"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_5c411afd913d49e7902f03ef","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 54 lines\n1: I’ll apply the delegated rulings, remove the optional DAG from the executable plan, add the invariant/compliance mapping and cross-host sharing tests, then sign off the design for B0.\n2: Completed-GMT: 2026-09-21 19:09:18 GMT  \n3: Completed-Local: 2026-09-22 02:09:18 +07  \n4: Coding-Agent: codex  \n5: Session-ID: 01a0bacb-3b91-7190-8412-3f1e85bb552a\n6: \n7: SIGN-OFF: READY to commit as a design document and begin B0.\n8: \n9: ```text\n10: +----+----------+------------------------------------------------------+\n11: | ID | Ruling   | Reason                                               |\n12: +----+----------+------------------------------------------------------+\n13: | D1 | ADOPT    | Recursive normalized comparison preserves observable  |\n14: |    |          | parity without comparing representation details.      |\n15: | D2 | ADOPT    | Drop the redundant lossless DAG. Named datoms remain  |\n16: |    |          | authoritative; lexical data is derived.              |\n17: | D3 | ADOPT    | Descriptor, raw-Bytes slots, lift, host classification|\n18: |    |          | and refusal rules satisfy portability boundaries.     |\n19: | D4 | MODIFY   | Fix the named VM leak separately; retain fixture      |\n20: |    |          | restrictions until that change exists.               |\n21: | D5 | ADOPT    | H is the canonical executable-image hash of a         |\n22: |    |          | definition unit or recursive component.               |\n23: | D6 | ADOPT    | Value/stream authority, verified loading, SCC hashing |\n24: |    |          | and stream failure events preserve the axioms.        |\n25: | D7 | ADOPT    | Both VMs coexist; benchmark results are informational |\n26: |    |          | and semantic-VM retirement requires its own design.   |\n27: +----+----------+------------------------------------------------------+\n28: ```\n29: \n30: The owner’s invariant is confirmed: the projection supplies alpha-equivalence\n31: identity, while the named-datom-derived executable image supplies\n32: alpha-invariant, content-addressed code that can cross `dao.stream`. The\n33: projection records themselves are not executable because they intentionally\n34: lose runtime-relevant information.\n35: \n36: The compliance table now covers the owner invariant, all datom.world\n37: non-negotiables, stream boundaries, continuation data, host refusal behavior,\n38: and the explicit strains around frames, loaded images, and diagnostic\n39: side-data.\n40: \n41: Sections changed:\n42: \n43: - §1: owner invariant, compliance table, three-artifact model, and D2 removal.\n44: - §2: scalar-class recording and host refusal.\n45: - §4: continuation and loaded-state boundaries.\n46: - §6: B0 criteria and B3/B4 behavior.\n47: - §7: cross-host sharing and stream-transfer tests.\n48: - §7.3: lossless DAG converted to a non-goal.\n49: - §8: replaced open decisions with DECIDED D1-D7 and DEFERRED mechanics.\n50: \n51: No ruling was rejected. D4 was modified to keep its implementation outside the\n52: B phases. No owner decision blocks B0; deferred work is limited to B6 request\n53: mechanics, contract stamps, SCC manifests, retry/timeout event vocabulary, and\n54: optional source-lineage data."}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"7dec4c58-c89b-4eb3-a17f-cbf151032121"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, let me verify the claims against the auxiliary files: the projection design, semantic VM sections 1-2, linearize.cljc, code.cljc, content.cljc. I could launch the explore agent, or I could just read them directly. The review asks for concrete verification. Let me read the main files (at least skim them) myself. These could be large. First check the sizes, then read the projection doc and skim the source files for the cited claims: `resolve-name`, `vector-operand-table`, `load-vector`, `:code-aliases`, `:yin.code/hash`, `segment-scope`, `closure-ranges`, `layout-conforms?`, `jing/segment-key`, `bind-params`, `resolve-var`, `yin.k/requires`.\n\nAlso check formatting issues: 80 columns, em dashes, status lines, model routing text. The design doc contains URLs but that's fine. Check for em dashes: \"Cross-\\nhost\" — lines 160-161 \"Cross- host\" is a hyphen split across lines so that's fine. Look for \"—\" in the design doc. Line 261 \"UCF remains proposed\" — fine.\n\nLet me grep for em dashes and check line lengths.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me verify claims against the supporting sources and check for